tive_function`. You can simply delete the lines of the second definition (lines 21-31) since they replicate the functionality of the first definition.\n\n### Additional Recommendations\n\n- **Ensure All Classes Are Imported:**\n  - It appears that the `Particle` class is not imported in `pso.py`. Make sure to include it to avoid runtime errors.\n  \n- **Implement Missing Methods:**\n  - The `update_velocity` method seems to be mentioned in the logic but is not defined in the provided code. You must define the logic for updating the particles\' velocities based on their personal best and global best positions.\n\n- **Add Comments to All Methods:**\n  - Ensure that all methods within the `PSO` class have complete and clear docstrings explaining their functionality, parameters, and return values.\n\n- **Test Cases:**\n  - Consider adding a separate test file to validate the functionality of the PSO implementation and the objective function, ensuring that the algorithm performs as expected.\n\n### Summary of Actions\n\n1. Remove the duplicate definition of `objective_function` in `utils.py`.\n2. Import the `Particle` class in `pso.py`.\n3. Implement the `update_velocity` method in the `PSO` class.\n4. Add comments for all methods and ensure clarity.\n5. Consider implementing test cases.\n\nBy addressing these issues, the implementation will adhere closely to the user\'s requirements and be more robust and user-friendly.Modality: Code.\nProgramming Language: Python.\nTask: Please refer to the input from user.\nComments on Codes: Please refer to the input from Reviewer.\nSource Code: You MUST firstly call the functions to load the source codes.\nAs the Programmer, to satisfy the new user\'s demand and make the software creative, executive and robust, you should modify corresponding codes according to the comments. Then, update the codes with all bugs fixed based on the comments. You should call the provided functions to save your modification. You should modify existing c